WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:11.250
Today we're gonna talk about [BEEP]. What did I say? [BEEP]
Look I know [BEEP] is technically a four-letter

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:15.809
word but how are we ever gonna get out
of [BEEP] if we can't even say the word [BEEP] ...DEBT!

00:00:15.809 --> 00:00:18.260
Ha! Gotcha!

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:19.800
Among the subjects that people like to

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:24.630
talk about debt ranks right between
toenail fungus and other people's dreams

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:29.340
your brain just doesn't want to hear it.
And that emotional reaction is partly

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:31.220
why it's so hard to pay it off.

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:32.520
But there's hope!

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:38.200
Researchers have figured out a way to
rewire your brain into taking this monster head-on.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:41.370
Not only can it save
your finances it can teach you a lot

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:45.520
about how your brain works
and maybe in the future you won't have

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:46.900
to be afraid of the word [BEEP].

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.580
Oh, come on!

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:02.420
The average debt holder in America
currently holds about $8,000 in credit

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:06.050
card debt over three cards! $26,000 in student loans

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:10.490
another $10,000 in car loans. That's a lot
for one person to manage and they all

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:15.410
have different interest rates, terms and
loan balances. In short it's a bit of a

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:17.960
confusing mess. So how do we get started?

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.280
First things first

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:24.770
you've got to mind the gap. The gap is
the difference between what you make and

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:29.270
spend in a month. Without a gap there's
no money available to make any kind of

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:34.369
progress. The two ways to widen your gap
are more income or less spending.

00:01:34.369 --> 00:01:38.960
Hopefully it's a mixture of both. Once
you have a gap to work with it's time to

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:40.440
think of strategy.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:42.920
If you ask a
mathematician how to structure your debt

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:47.630
they'd probably recommend something like
the Avalanche approach. You list your

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:51.740
debts by interest rates with the highest
at the top and the lowest at the bottom.

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:55.580
You pay minimums on everything except
the loan with the highest interest rate

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:59.570
which gets the biggest part of your gap.
Once that one's paid off you use the

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:04.009
increased cash flow to move down the
hillside like an avalanche. By the time

00:02:04.009 --> 00:02:07.340
you get to the bottom you save the most
money because you paid as little

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:12.110
interest as possible!
It's mathematical it's logical and it

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:13.580
doesn't work very well...

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:15.340
The Avalanche approach may be

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:21.350
mathematically sound but it omits one
important factor... your brain! Humans

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:25.760
aren't robots or Vulcans they're
emotional beings. They get discouraged,

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:30.470
they get overwhelmed, they have trouble
staying on course. It's the same reason

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:35.630
why those debt consolidation plans can
be a bad idea. It may seem like you're

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:40.160
simplifying your life to put all of your
loans into one big basket but what it

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:44.690
really does is create a giant
hulking dead monster that feels so

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:47.720
intimidating your brain just gives up.

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:49.760
So is there a method that works with your

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:54.320
brain's psychology instead of against it?
Well it turns out...

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:56.600
Julia we're in the

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:57.340
middle of something here.

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:59.239
I know I was
about to put it down but then I cleared

00:02:59.239 --> 00:03:03.290
a boss stage and upgraded the frosting
on my cupcake cannon I think I can get

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:05.580
to the persimmon palace by bedtime!

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:07.480
Turns out the same mind-control techniques

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:09.980
found in video games can work with your
finances.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:11.860
Game designers strategically

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:16.069
dole out positive reinforcements.
Clearing a board of gems, upgrading your

00:03:16.069 --> 00:03:19.970
loot which floods your brain with
pleasurable dopamine and keeps you

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:24.220
playing. At first these rewards are
handed out easily and often to get you

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:28.700
hooked and then more spaced out and
difficult as time goes by. It's really

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:33.920
effective and a little bit evil but the
same brain hacking technique can be used

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:38.870
to pay off your debt.
It's called the "Snowball Method". Instead

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:43.250
of listing your debts by interest rate
we list them by balance. Like the

00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:47.150
Avalanche approach you pay minimums on
all of them except you focus your

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:52.730
firepower on the smallest balance. Once
that's wiped out you roll the extra cash

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:58.340
down the hill to the next highest
balance and so on and so on. The snowball

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:04.040
method ensures you easy victories early
on to keep you motivated. Every time you

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:08.480
cross a debt off your list
it's like slaying a beast and upgrading

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:13.010
your weapon. Your brain will keep
chasing that dopamine fix even as the

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:16.389
levels get more challenging.

00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:20.200
[MUSIC]

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:24.140
While someone using the snowball method
will technically pay more overall

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:27.770
interest than someone using the
Avalanche approach that assumes that

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:32.630
they're both going to see it through. But
a study by Northwestern University found

00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:36.650
that snow ballers were much more likely
to actually stick with the plan and

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:40.820
successfully eliminate their debt even
if they owed more money than the

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:44.600
Avalanchers. Because they gave
themselves that dopamine edge...

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:45.720
Oh, [BEEP]!

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:46.640
Julia...

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:48.940
Sorry.

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:53.720
No matter what method you use the
hardest part of getting out of debt is

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:57.950
often just starting. And it can get
lonely because, you know, people don't

00:04:57.950 --> 00:05:02.240
like to talk about it. But with
determination and planning you can turn

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:06.120
debt into something you don't want to
think about into something you don't have to .

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:09.360
And that's our two cents!

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:15.220
[MUSIC]

